---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
    title="phishfence"
    description="a phishing detection pipeline that explains its reasoning."
>
    <a href="/" class="text-sm">return home</a>
    <header class="mb-4 mt-4">
        <h4 class="text-sm text-gray-500">2025</h4>
        <h1 class="mb-4">phishfence</h1>
        <p>a phishing detection pipeline that explains its reasoning.</p>
        <div
            class="flex flex-wrap gap-x-4 gap-y-2 my-4 font-mono text-sm text-gray-500 lowercase"
        >
            <span class="rounded border border-[#333] px-2 py-0.5">python</span>
            <span class="rounded border border-[#333] px-2 py-0.5"
                >machine learning</span
            >
            <span class="rounded border border-[#333] px-2 py-0.5"
                >phishing</span
            >
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-4 text-sm">
            <a
                href="https://github.com/thomasha1310/phishfence"
                target="_blank"
            >
                github ↗
            </a>
            <a
                href="https://www.bu.edu/summer/files/2025/10/Pract-2_Group-12_Poster.pdf"
                target="_blank"
            >
                poster ↗
            </a>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 italic">
            built at the 2025 boston university research in science and
            engineering program.
        </p>
    </header>

    <article>
        <hr />
        <h2>motivation</h2>
        <p>
            Everyone knows that phishing emails are dangerous. We also know that
            machine learning can be used to detect them. However, most phishing
            detection pipelines are black boxes. They can tell you whether an
            email is phishing or not, but they don't explain why, forcing
            vulnerable individuals to blindly trust a model's output.
        </p>
        <p>
            At the BU RISE program, my partners and I built PhishFence to
            mitigate this problem by matching modern phishing classifiers on
            accuracy while also providing a human-readable explanation of its
            reasoning. This explanation is our primary research goal.
        </p>
        <p>
            Of course, a well-designed pipeline is only as good as the data it
            is trained on. My work focused on curating a dataset and processing
            it into a workable format for training and evaluation.
        </p>

        <hr />
        <h2>data preparation</h2>
        <p>
            The most well-known online corpus of emails is the Enron corpus,
            which consists of mostly legitimate emails. We also added several
            datasets to supplement the number of phishing emails, including the
            Nazario corpus, for a <a
                href="https://github.com/thomasha1310/phishfence/blob/main/data/input/SOURCES.md"
                target="_blank">total of six publicly available datasets</a
            >.
        </p>
        <p>
            Although we were able to obtain a CSV file of each dataset, we found
            that many of the datasets had extraneous columns that were either
            not shared with the other datasets or were irrelevant to our task.
            We trimmed each dataset to include only the <code>subject</code>, <code
                >body</code
            >, and <code>label</code> columns.
        </p>
        <p>
            All duplicate rows and rows with missing values were removed. The
            six trimmed datasets were then concatenated into a single file
            containing 82,138 entries, of which 39,527 (48%) were legitimate and
            42,611 (52%) were phishing. This roughly even split mitigates the
            risk of biasing the model toward one class or the other.
        </p>
        <p>
            After initial testing, we determined that the Enron corpus, which
            comprised 29,767 entries (roughly 36%) of the total dataset, was too
            large and was skewing the model's performance. In particular, when
            using LIME to explain model behaviors, the word "Enron" consistently
            appeared as a top indicator for legitimate emails. This, of course,
            corresponds with the fact that the Enron corpus as a whole is
            heavily biased toward legitimate emails containing the word "Enron".
        </p>
        <p>
            To prevent overfitting to our specific dataset, we replaced all
            instances of the word "Enron" in the original Enron corpus with a
            randomly selected company from the Fortune 500 list of companies
            before recompiling the full dataset. After further analysis, we
            concluded that this successfully mitigated the bias.
        </p>

        <hr />
        <h2>outcome</h2>
        <p>
            After training the model using an 80/20 train-test split, we found
            that our pipeline achieved 99.3% accuracy, with precision and recall
            both above 99%, matching the performance of leading classifiers.
            More importantly, our explanability layer was able to provide
            human-readable explanations for each classification decision.
        </p>
        <p>
            We presented our results at the RISE Poster Symposium, where we
            showcased a web app where a user pastes in an email for
            classification and explanation.
        </p>

        <hr />
        <h2>limitations</h2>
        <p>
            When building a dataset for phishing, one major consideration is the
            ratio of legitimate to phishing emails. <a
                href="https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/overfitting/imbalanced-datasets"
                target="_blank"
                >Class imbalance can lead to heavily skewed models</a
            >, but artificially balancing the dataset can also lead to
            prediction bias. In our case, we chose to use a larger set of
            phishing emails to better capture the diverse nature of phishing
            attacks. As a result, we must understand that the resulting model
            may be biased toward predicting phishing emails.
        </p>
        <p>
            Additionally, our final pipeline makes use of Google's BERT language
            model, which can only read 512 tokens at a time. This means that we
            had to chunk longer emails, causing degraded context understanding
            and potentially decreased accuracy for longer emails.
        </p>
        <p>
            Our dataset also present inherent limitations: all the emails are in
            English, and the training corpus is static. This means that the
            model may not generalize well to other languages or to new phishing
            techniques that emerge after the dataset was compiled. As a
            proof-of-concept, PhishFence is a strong starting point, but it is
            not a production-ready system.
        </p>

        <hr />
        <h2>future work</h2>
        <p>
            Future research could focus on expanding the training data to
            include more languages and more recent phishing attacks.
            Additionally, the model could be improved to handle longer emails
            without chunking, perhaps by using a different architecture or by
            fine-tuning BERT for longer sequences. Finally, user studies could
            be conducted to evaluate the effectiveness of PhishFence's
            explanations in helping users make informed decisions about
            potential phishing emails.
        </p>

        <hr />
        <div class="flex gap-4 text-sm">
            <a
                href="https://github.com/thomasha1310/phishfence"
                target="_blank"
            >
                github ↗
            </a>
            <a
                href="https://www.bu.edu/summer/files/2025/10/Pract-2_Group-12_Poster.pdf"
                target="_blank"
            >
                poster ↗
            </a>
            <a href="/">return home </a>
        </div>
    </article>
</Layout>
